    <tags>developer-tools local-development postgresql redis mongodb nats caddy mysql elasticsearch rust windows development-environment</tags>
    <summary>Native Windows local development environment manager</summary>
    <description>
      Burrow runs services from one burrow.toml as native Windows processes, with
      automatic ports, generated environment variables, health-checked startup,
      verified package downloads, snapshots, and a live terminal dashboard. It does
      not require Docker, a virtual machine, or WSL.

      Features:
      - One burrow.toml to define your entire local stack
      - Native Windows processes (no Docker/WSL required for native services)
      - Automatic port allocation with stable per-project ports
      - Health-gated service startup with dependency graph
      - Verified package downloads with SHA-256 verification
      - Loopback reverse proxy with *.localhost routing and WebSocket passthrough
      - Live terminal dashboard (burrow top)
      - Full-directory snapshots with journaled restore
      - Read-only diagnostics (burrow doctor) with remedies

      Built-in packages: PostgreSQL 16, NATS 2.15, Caddy 2.11, MongoDB 7, Redis 7, MySQL 8, Elasticsearch 8
      Extensible via custom registry entries or container fallback.
    </description>
    <releaseNotes>
      Initial release of Burrow v0.1.0
      - Native Windows process supervision with Job Objects
      - Built-in packages: PostgreSQL 16, NATS 2.15, Caddy 2.11
      - Additional packages: MongoDB 7, Redis 7, MySQL 8, Elasticsearch 8
      - Loopback reverse proxy with *.localhost routing
      - Verified package downloads with SHA-256
      - Content-addressed cache with resumable downloads
      - Live terminal dashboard (burrow top)
      - Diagnostics with burrow doctor
      - Full-directory snapshots with restore
    </releaseNotes>
    <copyright>Copyright (c) 2024 vanshcodeworks</copyright>
    <license type="expression">MIT OR Apache-2.0</license>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
  </metadata>
  <files>
